#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Muscle Exercise Recommender
============================
Ask the user for ANY muscle (not a fixed menu) and print the best exercises
for that muscle, ranked BEST -> WORST, with the scientific/biomechanical
reasoning for why each one works the muscle and why it sits where it does
in the ranking.
 
The program recognizes common names, anatomical (Latin) names, and
everyday nicknames for 34 major skeletal muscles/muscle groups -- covering
essentially every muscle a person would train in the gym. If you type
something it doesn't recognize, it will try a fuzzy match, and otherwise
tell you it doesn't have that one yet (rather than pretending it does).
 
Ranking principles used throughout (established exercise-science /
biomechanics concepts, not a citation of one single study):
  1. Stretch-mediated tension - loading a muscle while it is lengthened
     tends to produce a stronger growth/strength stimulus than loading it
     only in a shortened position.
  2. Resistance profile - whether the exercise keeps tension on the muscle
     through its full range (e.g., cables) or loses tension at certain
     joint angles (e.g., free weights at the "easy" point of the arc).
  3. Direct vs. shared tension - how much of the work goes to the target
     muscle vs. to synergists/stabilizers that may fatigue first.
  4. Range of motion available at the joint(s) involved.
  5. Stability demands - instability can divert effort away from the
     target muscle and into balance/stabilization instead.
  6. Motor unit recruitment - heavier loads and movements capable of
     producing high force output recruit a greater proportion of
     high-threshold (fast-twitch) motor units, which carry the greatest
     potential for strength and size gains.
  7. Progressive overload - how easily and how far an exercise's
     difficulty (load, reps, or range) can keep increasing over time;
     exercises with a higher ceiling for continued overload sustain
     progress longer than ones that are quickly capped (e.g. by
     bodyweight or a light cable stack).
"""
 
